WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.780
Welcome to this deep dive. And if you're listening to this right now, you are likely a new engineer

00:04.780 --> 00:10.600
stepping into the Azure SPI machinery. Yeah. And first of all, welcome. It's a complex space,

00:10.880 --> 00:15.540
but, you know, highly rewarding once you get your bearings. Exactly. So our mission today is your

00:15.540 --> 00:21.000
orientation. Before you get lost in the weeds of like specific microservices or deployment

00:21.000 --> 00:26.600
manifests, we want to give you the complete high level map of the territory. Right. Because we're

00:26.600 --> 00:31.380
pulling from three core architecture documents today, right? The complete guide, the engineering

00:31.380 --> 00:35.940
system guide and the SPI stack guide. Yeah. We're synthesizing all of that to give you the foundation.

00:36.140 --> 00:42.900
And to start, I want you to imagine standing in an archive that is roughly the size of a major

00:42.900 --> 00:49.260
metropolitan city. Oh, wow. OK. That's a big archive. Huge. And it's filled with decades of incredibly

00:49.260 --> 00:54.860
valuable records, you know, seismic surveys, well logs, drilling measurements, temperature readings

00:54.860 --> 01:01.100
from deep underground. But here is the massive catch. Every single aisle of this archives is

01:01.100 --> 01:04.300
written in a completely different, totally incompatible language.

01:04.300 --> 01:10.940
Right. Because in the energy industry, a vendor in 1990 had a completely different technical definition

01:10.940 --> 01:14.800
of a well than, say, a vendor doing a survey in 2010.

01:15.220 --> 01:20.320
Exactly. So if you try to merge two oil and gas companies, you have to somehow merge these two

01:20.320 --> 01:26.700
massive incompatible libraries. It is a data translation problem on just an industrial scale.

01:27.060 --> 01:31.680
And, you know, for a long time, the industry's answer was just writing endless fragile adapters.

01:31.680 --> 01:37.240
I mean, an engineering team would have to build a brand new custom translation layer just to get a

01:37.240 --> 01:41.780
new analysis tool to read the old data. Which is an integration nightmare. Millions of dollars,

01:41.940 --> 01:47.540
thousands of hours. But the solution to that nightmare is OSDU, right? The open subsurface data

01:47.540 --> 01:52.440
universe. Yes. It acts as the industry's universal translator. It's an open standard that basically

01:52.440 --> 01:57.940
says, from now on, we all agree on exactly what a well is, and we all use the exact same APIs to find

01:57.940 --> 02:03.000
it. Right. But before we go a single step further into how Microsoft makes that run on error, I want

02:03.000 --> 02:07.780
to clarify a major point of confusion right out of the gate. Oh, the acronyms. Yeah. This trips up almost

02:07.780 --> 02:13.960
every new engineer. Yeah. As you read these internal docs, you're going to see the acronym SPI

02:13.960 --> 02:21.180
used in three completely different ways. And if you don't separate them now, the architecture will

02:21.180 --> 02:27.240
make zero sense. You really have to compartmentalize them. First, there is the interface. That refers to

02:27.240 --> 02:34.960
OSDU's original service provider interface. Think of it as the seam in the community code between the

02:34.960 --> 02:39.780
shared business logic and the cloud-specific plumbing. Right. The actual boundary in the code.

02:39.780 --> 02:45.180
Second, there is the stack. This is the Azure project officially called OSDU Spy Stack. That is

02:45.180 --> 02:49.380
the deployment system that spins up the actual Azure infrastructure. And finally, you have the

02:49.380 --> 02:55.240
engineering system, or OSDU Spy. That is the machinery that keeps the Azure code synchronized with a broader

02:55.240 --> 03:00.160
open source community project. So you've got the code, the system that synchronizes it, and the platform

03:00.160 --> 03:04.820
that runs it. Let's start with the code, because OSDU is built on a provider model, right? Yeah. You have

03:04.820 --> 03:09.420
shared business logic, like say the partition service, which figures out which tenant is making a

03:09.420 --> 03:15.040
request. That logic is identical everywhere. But underneath it, you have swappable cloud plumbing

03:15.040 --> 03:22.000
for AWS, Google Cloud, and Azure. And every cloud handles those fundamental tasks differently. Azure

03:22.000 --> 03:28.500
relies on Cosmos DB for NoSQL storage and Service Bus for messaging, while AWS might use DynamoDB.

03:28.740 --> 03:34.900
Right. So the Azure provider code isn't just some tiny configuration file. It is real, complex plumbing

03:34.900 --> 03:41.520
that translates OSDU's generic requests into highly optimized Cosmos DB queries.

03:41.520 --> 03:47.320
Which leads us to this existential threat Azure faces. Because upstream OSDU, the central community

03:47.320 --> 03:52.520
project, is permanently deleting all vendor-specific provider code from their own repositories.

03:52.520 --> 03:57.460
And what's fascinating here is that this completely inverts the normal problem you face when you fork an

03:57.460 --> 04:00.280
open source project. Right. Normally you worry about them changing things.

04:00.280 --> 04:05.040
Exactly. Usually you create a fork and your biggest headache is that the upstream project keeps

04:05.040 --> 04:08.400
changing the code you depend on. So you have to constantly merge their updates.

04:08.400 --> 04:12.520
A normal fork is basically just a snapshot in time that slowly drifts away.

04:13.060 --> 04:18.860
But here, Azure has to contend with upstream deliberately deleting the very code they depend

04:18.860 --> 04:19.440
on. Yeah.

04:19.600 --> 04:25.160
If Azure just stops tracking upstream, they stop being OSDU. They become a stagnant archive.

04:25.160 --> 04:31.400
No security patches, no new features. But if they manually merge upstream's changes every day...

04:31.400 --> 04:37.320
They face endless conflicts because the Azure-specific files literally do not exist in the upstream repository

04:37.320 --> 04:43.760
anymore. Okay, let's unpack this. How do you continually merge daily updates into a repository where

04:43.760 --> 04:48.340
half the files have been deliberately deleted by upstream without breaking everything?

04:48.900 --> 04:53.640
You have to fundamentally change how ownership works in Git. The boundary of ownership doesn't go around the

04:53.640 --> 04:56.020
whole repository anymore. It runs through the tree.

04:56.220 --> 04:57.140
Meaning what exactly?

04:57.420 --> 05:01.060
It means sitting right there in the same Git repository at the exact same commit,

05:01.640 --> 05:05.800
Azure permanently owns some folders, specifically the Azure provider implementation.

05:06.520 --> 05:09.060
Meanwhile, upstream still owns the core shared modules.

05:09.460 --> 05:13.660
So you have two distinct masters controlling different sub-directories within a single repo.

05:14.600 --> 05:17.440
Which brings us to the engineering system, OSDU-SPY.

05:17.760 --> 05:20.840
Right. Because if you're a developer trying to fix a daily conflict,

05:20.840 --> 05:24.680
the naive approach is just running git merge-x theirs.

05:24.940 --> 05:28.820
Yeah, you just tell Git to prefer the upstream changes whenever there is a dispute.

05:29.140 --> 05:34.380
But that fails miserably because of what Git calls a modified delete conflict.

05:34.920 --> 05:40.420
Say, upstream modifies a core file, but also deletes an old AWS provider folder.

05:40.500 --> 05:40.820
Uh-huh.

05:41.100 --> 05:43.880
Well, your Azure fork has already deleted that AWS folder.

05:43.880 --> 05:49.280
So Git looks at this and just throws its hands up. It stops the automated merge and asks a human,

05:49.640 --> 05:52.800
do you want this new modification or do you want to keep this folder deleted?

05:52.860 --> 05:56.660
And because the sync has to happen daily, a human engineer is going to get that exact same

05:56.660 --> 05:58.960
unanswerable prompt tomorrow and the next day.

05:59.080 --> 06:04.140
Exactly. And human nature being what it is, a tired developer at 5 p.m. will often just type

06:04.140 --> 06:07.020
git add dash a to force the merge through.

06:07.360 --> 06:08.140
Oh, no.

06:08.140 --> 06:12.340
Yeah. And by doing that, they silently restore all the files upstream intended to delete.

06:13.400 --> 06:15.940
The careful isolation is quietly undone.

06:16.340 --> 06:20.660
So to prevent that nightmare, the engineering system enforces a golden rule.

06:21.320 --> 06:23.900
Generate the branch. Do not merge into it.

06:24.180 --> 06:27.480
This specific mechanism is the fulcrum of the entire architecture.

06:28.500 --> 06:33.760
The branch absorbing upstream changes doesn't actually need to be a chronological history of human merges.

06:33.760 --> 06:38.460
It can just be a pure mathematical function of the upstream tip and a configuration file.

06:38.620 --> 06:41.260
But I'm going to need you to break down the actual mechanics of that.

06:41.620 --> 06:45.200
If you aren't doing a textual merge, how are you combining the code?

06:45.480 --> 06:47.300
So it comes down to Git internals.

06:48.040 --> 06:51.780
Git tracks files as blobs of data and directories as trees.

06:52.280 --> 06:57.060
We usually interact with Git by diffing lines of text, but this system drops down a level.

06:57.180 --> 06:59.240
Okay. So skipping the text comparison entirely.

06:59.240 --> 07:03.540
Right. It uses low-level plumbing commands, specifically Git read tree.

07:03.960 --> 07:08.820
It reads the upstream code, runs a custom filter engine over it to strip out the non-Azure providers,

07:09.100 --> 07:12.060
and stitches together a brand new tree object in memory.

07:12.320 --> 07:14.100
And then it stamps a brand new commit on it.

07:14.460 --> 07:19.960
Because no textual merge algorithm ever runs, a modified delete conflict literally cannot occur.

07:20.280 --> 07:21.260
That is fascinating.

07:21.260 --> 07:26.860
It's like rebuilding a custom car engine from scratch every single morning according to a blueprint,

07:27.180 --> 07:31.120
rather than trying to patch yesterday's oil leak over and over again.

07:31.200 --> 07:32.820
The blueprint analogy is perfect.

07:33.000 --> 07:33.120
Yeah.

07:33.260 --> 07:37.100
And to manage this assembly line, they use three very specific branches.

07:37.760 --> 07:39.060
First is fork upstream.

07:39.500 --> 07:41.320
Which is the purely generated branch rate.

07:41.440 --> 07:42.680
No human ever commits to it.

07:42.720 --> 07:44.980
Right. Nothing but the filter engine ever writes to it.

07:45.900 --> 07:47.500
Then there's fork integration.

07:47.760 --> 07:48.200
The workspace.

07:48.200 --> 07:55.320
Yes. That's where the pristine generated upstream tree is finally combined with the fork-owned Azure source code.

07:55.640 --> 08:00.760
That's where developers resolve legitimate, logical code conflicts or update versions.

08:01.360 --> 08:02.460
It is allowed to break.

08:02.860 --> 08:05.440
And finally, you have main, which represents production.

08:05.920 --> 08:09.880
Heavily protected, only receiving pull requests that have already passed all validations.

08:10.020 --> 08:15.980
And we should take a moment to highlight just how incredibly strict that filter engine is when it builds the fork upstream branch.

08:15.980 --> 08:19.240
Oh, right. Because it categorizes every single top-level directory.

08:19.600 --> 08:26.000
Yeah. If it encounters a file or folder, it doesn't recognize, say, upstream adds a brand new folder for an experimental service.

08:26.200 --> 08:28.520
It does not attempt to guess what it should do.

08:28.740 --> 08:34.360
It just immediately halts the entire sync process with an exit code, too, and demands human intervention.

08:34.620 --> 08:34.820
Right.

08:35.040 --> 08:37.480
Which, I can see developers getting annoyed by that.

08:37.580 --> 08:40.580
It seems almost painfully strict for an automated daily sync.

08:40.980 --> 08:44.980
Why not just default to keeping unknown files just to keep the pipeline moving?

08:44.980 --> 08:51.160
Because in complex systems, guessing is convenient exactly once and fatal forever after.

08:51.260 --> 08:52.060
Ah, I see.

08:52.240 --> 08:58.180
If the system defaults to dropping unknown things, a crucial new upstream module gets silently deleted.

08:58.640 --> 09:01.920
And a week later, your build fails for some mysterious reason.

09:02.120 --> 09:08.220
And if it defaults to keeping unknown things, upstream's proprietary code quietly bloats your Azure fork.

09:08.880 --> 09:12.780
Demanding a loud failure over a quiet assumption isn't just a technical rule here.

09:12.860 --> 09:14.240
It is the core philosophy.

09:14.240 --> 09:14.680
Exactly.

09:15.060 --> 09:17.640
But, you know, code sitting in a repository is useless.

09:18.100 --> 09:18.280
Right.

09:18.500 --> 09:23.340
Which brings us to the deployment system, the stack, or OSU-SMI stack.

09:23.560 --> 09:30.680
Because if this plumbing is heavily tied to Cosmos DB and Azure Service Bus, you can't just run a local unit test on a laptop.

09:31.120 --> 09:33.120
Unit tests are wholly insufficient here.

09:33.480 --> 09:40.140
To prove the Azure provider code works, you need all of those databases, messaging queues, and search clusters actually running.

09:40.140 --> 09:45.220
And standing all of that infrastructure up by hand would be a multi-day nightmare in the Azure portal.

09:45.940 --> 09:54.500
But the stack turns an empty Azure subscription into a fully running OSDU environment in roughly 50 minutes using a single command to spy up.

09:54.500 --> 09:56.160
It is incredibly comprehensive.

09:56.160 --> 10:05.120
That one command provisions the Kubernetes cluster, spins up five distinct Azure data services, installs middleware, and deploys 10 different OSDU core services.

10:05.220 --> 10:09.440
And it seeds the initial test data so the APIs can actually answer real requests immediately.

10:09.440 --> 10:13.020
Which highlights what the docs call the Azure-only bet.

10:13.340 --> 10:13.460
Right.

10:13.600 --> 10:20.480
The deliberate choice to use managed Azure services instead of portable in-cluster equivalents like PostgreSQL or RabbitNQ.

10:20.720 --> 10:27.900
And I have to push back here because, wait, isn't the whole point of cloud-native to run everything inside Kubernetes so it's perfectly portable?

10:28.180 --> 10:33.620
Well, if we connect this to the bigger picture, you have to look at what they are actually trying to test.

10:33.760 --> 10:33.960
Okay.

10:34.100 --> 10:35.440
Testing the Azure-specific stuff.

10:35.440 --> 10:36.320
Right.

10:36.520 --> 10:41.440
The stack exists primarily to prove that the Azure provider code functions correctly.

10:42.180 --> 10:45.880
Cosmos DB behaves differently than a generic PostgreSQL container.

10:46.200 --> 10:51.140
So testing against generic databases would prove absolutely nothing about how it runs in reality.

10:51.500 --> 10:54.280
Portability is actively counterproductive to their goal.

10:54.560 --> 10:55.000
Exactly.

10:55.480 --> 11:02.880
You want the test environment tightly coupled to Azure-specific quirks because that tight coupling is the sole reason the code exists.

11:02.880 --> 11:07.400
And the documentation is careful to state this is explicitly not a production configuration, right?

11:07.480 --> 11:08.300
Oh, absolutely not.

11:08.440 --> 11:11.820
For speed and cost, everything shares a single managed identity.

11:12.280 --> 11:14.420
There's no multi-region disaster recovery.

11:15.000 --> 11:19.180
It is highly tuned for rapid testing, not for running a live oil company.

11:19.180 --> 11:26.940
So we have the engineering system generating code and the stack providing a massive 50-minute deployment to run it.

11:27.900 --> 11:34.120
How do these two completely independent systems actually interact without grinding development to a halt?

11:34.520 --> 11:36.560
Well, this interaction is what they call the handshake.

11:36.740 --> 11:36.980
Mm-hmm.

11:36.980 --> 11:43.680
Because remember, you have eight different OSDU service forks, partition, entitlements legal, and so on.

11:43.820 --> 11:51.020
And every single time a developer opens a pull request, their code needs to be tested against a live environment before it merges.

11:51.280 --> 11:51.480
Right.

11:51.620 --> 11:53.800
But as you mentioned, speedup takes 50 minutes.

11:53.800 --> 12:00.700
You can't spend 50 minutes spinning up a brand new cluster and paying for a whole new Cosmos DB account for every single PR.

12:01.020 --> 12:03.780
That would be insanely slow and astronomically expensive.

12:03.880 --> 12:05.240
Which is why they use a shared environment.

12:05.240 --> 12:09.440
There is one standing, fully provisioned OSDU environment running all the time.

12:09.660 --> 12:16.620
Okay, so when a pull request needs to run its tests, it essentially borrows a slot in that shared environment, proves its image works, and then gives the slot back.

12:17.180 --> 12:18.520
But here's where I get stuck.

12:18.580 --> 12:19.500
Okay, what's the hangup?

12:19.760 --> 12:26.640
If I'm testing my new storage service and you're testing your new partition service in the exact same environment at the exact same time,

12:26.940 --> 12:31.340
how are our deployments not constantly colliding and ruining each other's test results?

12:31.340 --> 12:39.440
Ah, they avoid collisions through a highly elegant get-offs mechanism using a tool called FluxCD.

12:40.040 --> 12:40.560
FluxCD?

12:40.760 --> 12:44.280
Yeah, it's essentially a robotic operator sitting inside the Kubernetes cluster.

12:44.640 --> 12:53.100
It continuously watches a configuration repository, and its only job is to ensure the live cluster exactly matches that declarative state.

12:53.100 --> 12:58.720
So instead of a developer trying to manually run cubicle commands, they just update the configuration repo?

12:58.940 --> 12:59.200
Almost.

12:59.880 --> 13:01.460
They update a very specific piece of it.

13:01.920 --> 13:08.320
When a pull request wants to test a new image, it writes to a single Kubernetes config map called OSDU image lock.

13:08.420 --> 13:09.280
Yeah, image lock.

13:09.480 --> 13:11.100
Okay, let's bring back our car analogy.

13:11.320 --> 13:11.800
Let's hear it.

13:11.800 --> 13:21.280
If the engineering system generates a new engine from a blueprint every morning, this get-offs lock file is like borrowing one specific slot on a running engine test bench.

13:21.680 --> 13:29.860
You drop in your custom untested spark plug, prove that it fires correctly, and then you put the standard spark plug right back so the next team can use the bench.

13:29.860 --> 13:32.380
That captures the transient nature of it perfectly.

13:32.880 --> 13:39.280
The pipeline runs a script that pins its ephemeral, untested container image into that lock file.

13:39.540 --> 13:45.140
And FluxCD immediately notices the change and rolls out the new image only for that specific service.

13:45.240 --> 13:45.600
Exactly.

13:46.040 --> 13:49.540
The other 13 OSDU services are left completely untouched.

13:49.880 --> 13:52.820
The developer runs their tests against the live APIs.

13:52.820 --> 13:57.680
And when the tests finish, pass or fail, the pipeline restores the lock file.

13:58.220 --> 13:59.540
Borrow, prove, restore.

13:59.840 --> 14:01.860
Here's where it gets really interesting, though.

14:02.160 --> 14:03.180
The security design.

14:03.380 --> 14:03.960
Oh, it's brilliant.

14:04.340 --> 14:12.780
Because these are eight completely separate GitHub repositories that do not inherently trust each other, yet they all deploy into one shared cluster.

14:13.080 --> 14:18.060
Normally, you'd have to distribute some super powerful admin credential to all eight repos.

14:18.060 --> 14:22.500
If someone compromises even one, they have the keys to your entire cloud account.

14:22.500 --> 14:29.240
But in this handshake, there are zero stored credentials, no client secrets, no long-lived passwords waiting to be stolen.

14:29.520 --> 14:29.740
Wait.

14:30.100 --> 14:34.680
If there are no passwords, how does GitHub prove to Azure that it's allowed to change the lock file?

14:34.860 --> 14:39.360
They use OpenID Connect, OIDC, paired with a GitHub-protected environment.

14:39.980 --> 14:44.620
Instead of a password, they use cryptographic certificates to mathematically prove their identity.

14:44.720 --> 14:46.840
So the stack sets up a federated credential.

14:47.240 --> 14:48.800
How specific is that credential?

14:49.080 --> 14:50.000
Incredibly specific.

14:50.000 --> 14:50.480
Perfect.

14:50.480 --> 14:59.480
The subject line essentially says, is this request coming from this exact repository, and is it actively running inside a job that a reviewer explicitly approved?

14:59.680 --> 15:00.100
Wow.

15:00.600 --> 15:04.220
So the trust is expressed mathematically tied to the exact context.

15:04.420 --> 15:10.720
And even then, it only has permissions to patch that one specific OSDO image lock config map.

15:10.880 --> 15:11.140
Right.

15:11.380 --> 15:12.580
It cannot create new pods.

15:12.740 --> 15:14.000
It cannot delete resources.

15:14.280 --> 15:16.460
It has zero access to Kubernetes secrets.

15:16.460 --> 15:22.880
So even if a service fork was completely compromised by a bad actor, the worst they could do is change which image a service runs.

15:23.040 --> 15:25.280
They couldn't steal the Cosmos DB credentials.

15:25.420 --> 15:28.300
It is a master class in least privileged security design.

15:28.560 --> 15:32.740
So bringing this all together, what does this all mean for you as you dive into the code?

15:33.300 --> 15:38.220
You are stepping into an engineering system handling immense industry-scale complexity.

15:38.220 --> 15:43.200
But it doesn't just add more moving parts or paper over the mess with manual intervention.

15:43.420 --> 15:43.520
Right.

15:43.760 --> 15:46.820
It manages complexity through incredibly strict boundaries.

15:47.180 --> 15:50.740
It generates clean branches instead of chronologically merging.

15:51.120 --> 15:55.180
It utilizes clever GitOps mechanics to share a live environment safely.

15:55.440 --> 15:59.320
It's entirely about demanding strictness up front so you don't deal with chaos later.

15:59.540 --> 16:01.620
Which brings me to a final thought for you to chew on.

16:01.620 --> 16:08.040
We talk about the filter engine's absolute refusal to guess when it sees an unknown file halting with an exit code too.

16:08.260 --> 16:11.000
Yeah, that refusal to guess isn't just about Git repositories.

16:11.100 --> 16:12.760
It's about how we build software in general.

16:12.920 --> 16:13.400
Exactly.

16:13.900 --> 16:19.180
This entire SPI architecture is built on demanding loud failures over quiet assumptions.

16:19.920 --> 16:22.720
If the system doesn't know, it screams and stops.

16:22.900 --> 16:26.280
Unlike that giant messy archive of energy data we started with.

16:26.280 --> 16:26.740
Right.

16:27.060 --> 16:34.840
The only reason it got so messy is because localized systems quietly assumed they knew what the data meant rather than stopping and asking for a strict definition.

16:35.100 --> 16:35.760
That's a great point.

16:35.960 --> 16:44.880
So as you start writing your own code within this machinery, ask yourself, where in your own logic are you making quiet assumptions just to keep the pipeline moving?

16:45.260 --> 16:50.960
And how might embracing loud immediate failures actually build more trust and resilience in the long run?

16:51.420 --> 16:53.900
Welcome to the Azure SPI machinery.

16:54.380 --> 16:55.060
Happy exploring.

